This site is built with my preferred tech stack — picked for two things: speed, and how well it plays with coding agents.

## Vite Ecosystem

[**Vite**](https://vite.dev/) is the build tool underneath it all — it's fast, requires almost no config, and works with everything.

[**Vitest**](https://vitest.dev/) for unit and component tests.

[**oxlint**](https://oxc.rs/docs/guide/usage/linter.html) for linting and [**oxfmt**](https://oxc.rs/docs/guide/usage/formatter.html) for formatting — both use my own [**@julianelda/lexis**](https://github.com/JulianElda/arche/tree/master/packages/lexis) package, and both are faster than [**ESLint**](https://eslint.org/) and Prettier, which matters for a tighter agent feedback loop. I still keep ESLint around because oxlint doesn't fully support it yet.

## Tooling

[**Bun**](https://bun.com/) is the package manager and runtime. Claude Code runs on Bun, for what it's worth.

[**Storybook**](https://storybook.js.org/) for building and browsing components in isolation.

[**Lefthook**](https://lefthook.dev/) for git hooks — no Node startup per commit like Husky, and hooks run in parallel instead of one after another.

[**nano-staged**](https://github.com/usmanyunusov/nano-staged) auto-lints and auto-formats staged files on pre-commit. Leaner than [**lint-staged**](https://github.com/lint-staged/lint-staged).

```json
{
  "**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}": ["oxlint --fix", "oxfmt"],
  "**/*.{css,html,json,yaml,yml,md}": "oxfmt",
  "**/*.{svelte}": ["oxlint --fix", "oxfmt"]
}
```

[**commitlint**](https://github.com/conventional-changelog/commitlint) enforces conventional commit messages.

## Testing

[**Playwright**](https://playwright.dev/) for end-to-end tests. [**Playwright CLI**](https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-cli) also lets an agent open a browser and see the app directly.

[**@vitest/browser-playwright**](https://vitest.dev/guide/browser/) for running Vitest's browser-mode tests through
Playwright.

## Styling

[**Tailwind CSS**](https://tailwindcss.com/) for utility-first styling — once you're used to it, you can see a component's styles without jumping through multiple CSS files, and the media query syntax is intuitive enough that you stop thinking about breakpoint variable names.

## Agents

A couple of things for the agents.

**AGENTS.md** covers general code conventions — naming, TypeScript, Svelte, styling,
testing.

**CLAUDE.md** covers general architecture — project shape, content, API
structure. I prefer to ask the agent (I use Claude) to scan the repo and have it write the file.

**Claude Code hooks** in **.claude/settings.json** auto-lint and
auto-format after every tool use, so the produced code always meets the repo's lint
and formatting standards. It's also cheaper to run a script than to burn
AI tokens getting an agent to match the style by hand.

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Write|Edit|MultiEdit|Bash",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "cd $(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) && ./node_modules/.bin/nano-staged --unstaged --quiet --bail"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "Stop": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "cd $(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) && ./node_modules/.bin/nano-staged --unstaged --quiet --bail"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## On the horizon

[**Vite+**](https://viteplus.dev/) would fold oxfmt, oxlint, vite, vitest into a single library. [**Typescript 7.1**](https://github.com/microsoft/typescript-go/milestone/6) is expected to ship programmatic API for Svelte tooling that currently still need Typescript 6.